      <h2>Engineering Success</h2>
      <p>
        Engineering success is documented by following the engineering design cycle — Design → Build → Test → Learn — in a technical aspect of the project, and by stating how each round of results reshapes the next design. The PAGER-Yeast biosensor is built from four independent functional modules: chassis cell engineering (FAR1/SST2 knockout), Gpa1 humanization, the PAGER antigen-gated fusion receptor, and the FUS1 promoter reporter system. Each module is engineered through its own cycle(s); cycles are numbered within their module.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two complete DBTL cycles have been completed for the chassis module, taking the CRISPR-Cas9 knockout of FAR1 and SST2 from a failed first attempt to a plasmid-free ΔFAR1 ΔSST2 strain; two cycles have been completed for the humanized G-protein module, taking the GPA1-G&alpha;q chimera from a Gibson-assembly route that accumulated PCR mutations to an overlap-extension fusion PCR route that yielded correctly genotyped strains 12 and 17; four cycles have been completed for the reporter module, covering the validated yEGFP fluorescent reporter and three follow-up colorimetric designs (lacZ&alpha;, MEL1, and PRE1/2 promoter mutagenesis); and three cycles have been documented for the PAGER fusion receptor, which moved from a full-length construct that failed to express, to a truncated version that expressed but mislocalised, to a third version that isolates the ICL3 loop while keeping the native signal peptide.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div id="engineering-chassis" class="content-card">
      <h2>Chassis Cell Engineering — FAR1/SST2 Gene Knockout</h2>
        <p>
          Wild-type <i>Saccharomyces cerevisiae</i> BY4741 possesses a native mating pheromone response MAPK pathway. Two endogenous factors interfere with our synthetic human-origin GPCR (hM1Dq) signal transmission. FAR1 mediates cell-cycle G1 arrest upon pheromone stimulation, preventing continuous proliferation and long-term monitoring. SST2 acts as a negative regulator of G-protein signaling, dampening the signal amplitude of the GPCR–MAPK cascade. To re-wire the native pathway for our synthetic sensor, we performed targeted gene editing to remove growth arrest and enhance signal sensitivity, creating a chassis better suited for downstream human GPCR coupling.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two cycles were required: Cycle 1 tested two parallel assembly routes and failed on both; Cycle 2 redesigned the guide sequences, pre-assembled the sgRNA by inverse PCR, extended the repair-template homology arms, and produced the double-knockout chassis.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div id="engineering-chassis-cycle1" class="content-card">
      <h2>Cycle 1: Three-part Co-transformation and In Vitro One-step Cloning (Version 1)</h2>

    <h3 id="engineering-chassis-cycle1-design">Design</h3>
        <p>
          Two parallel routes were designed to construct a CRISPR-Cas9 knockout system targeting FAR1/SST2. Route A: linearized pML104 was assembled with the guide fragment in vitro via one-step cloning, and the circular plasmid was then transformed into yeast. Route B: linearized pML104, the guide fragment, and an approximately 600 bp repair template were co-transformed into yeast in a three-part manner, relying on endogenous homologous recombination in yeast to complete sgRNA assembly and gene knockout. Guide sequence were designed using Benchling and ranked by specificity and efficiency scores (Table 1 and Table 2). The final candidates were not simply the top-scoring sequences; target position within the coding sequence was also considered to ensure efficient disruption of gene function.
        </p>
