        <p class="eng-fig__cap">Table 4. Designed guide sequence for SST2 knockout (Cycle 2).</p>

    <h3 id="engineering-chassis-cycle2-build">Build</h3>
        <p>
          Using the empty pML104 plasmid as template, inverse PCR was performed with primers containing the guide sequence, and the linearized pML104-sgRNA was gel-purified (Figure 4). The upstream and downstream homology arms of FAR1/SST2 were amplified separately, and the repair templates were assembled by fusion PCR and gel-purified (Figure 5). Stepwise knockout: linearized pML104-sgRNA-FAR1 and the FAR1 repair template were co-transformed into BY4741; after colony PCR screening, ΔFAR1 BY4741 was obtained by 5-FOA counter-selection. Using ΔFAR1 as the chassis, linearized pML104-sgRNA-SST2 and the SST2 repair template were then co-transformed; after colony PCR screening, ΔFAR1 ΔSST2 BY4741 was obtained by 5-FOA counter-selection (Figure 6 and Figure 7).
        </p>

    <h3 id="engineering-chassis-cycle2-test">Test</h3>
        <p>
          Colony PCR screening yielded ΔFAR1-positive clones F1–F6, F8, and F9, and ΔSST2-positive clones S1, S5–S7, and S15 (Figure 8). Sanger sequencing confirmed a 4 bp deletion (TCTG) in SST2 and a 4 bp deletion (CACT) in FAR1, both causing frameshift mutations; non-knockout clones were consistent with the wild type (Figure 9). Fluorescence intensity measurements further confirmed the successful knockout of FAR1 and SST2. Under 1 μM α-factor induction, both the FAR1 single knockout and the FAR1/SST2 double knockout showed higher fluorescence intensity than the wild type. For the FAR1 knockout, the difference increased over time and became significant at 6 h (P=0.0240) and 8 h (P=0.0011). For the FAR1/SST2 double knockout strain, the fluorescence intensity was significantly higher than that of the wild type at all time points, with P=0.0099 at 2 h and P&lt;0.0001 at 4, 6, and 8 h. These results suggest that FAR1 and SST2 may negatively regulate the α-factor signaling pathway or the downstream fluorescence expression, and the double knockout had a stronger effect than the single knockout (Figure 10).
        </p>
